      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1172,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), and it is the reason water is such an excellent solvent.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking and washing.\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible gas).\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that are essential for life and the planet:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to carry nutrients and minerals through the body and transport energy in plants.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature, making life possible.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the \"lifeblood\" of the planet. It makes up about 60% of the human body and is essential for:\n*   Regulating body temperature.\n*   Lubricating joints.\n*   Transporting oxygen and nutrients to cells.\n*   Removing waste.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule ($H_2O$) with complex and vital properties that make life on Earth possible."
